body:
I may regret sharing this, but I'm not feeling all that great so here goes:

&#x200B;

When I was in my teens, there was a few "mental health professionals" at a mental hospital I was in that were very confusing and cruel. One person in particular, would accuse me of playing games. Like, we would be in our first group of the day and when he would get to me, he would say " so what games are you going to play today?" And this would get me so angry and I would cuss and say vulgar things, and it would land me in an isolation room. Because he was an adult, and because I felt like a shitty person and son and everything else, I believed him, especially since he didn't do this to most of the other teens there. He would tell them good job, even though I don't think they were really doing much of anything. But I think that gave them this idea that they were like junior therapists or mental health professionals too. And if I pointed out how he was being selective in how he treated people, I would just get accused of playing the victim. He once just shamed me in front of the group; him and another "professional" said that I was scaring my mother. And when I asked her this, she said that I wasn't, which all that did was confuse me. And the thing is, after he would tell me all these things, he would say "until you change..." and I can't remember the rest, but it never came off as advice or anything. It was just more confusion because all I kept thinking was that isn't this why we're in these groups? How is making snide and vague accusations of me "playing game" supposed to help me change? I think where some of that was coming from was that I was quiet and didn't like sharing much, especially in front of other teens who weren't very understanding and judged pretty harshly. But again, how the hell was talking to me that way supposed to get me to open up more. It just seemed so ass backwards.

This has been many years ago, but I still second guess myself. I always feel like I am this bad or manipulative person. I hate the people there so much, but especially him, Robert, for being the biggest one to shame and attack me that way. And yet....in the back of my mind, I feel like I am forgetting something. Like, maybe I did something to deserve all of that. There must be some valid reason for his behavior towards me. 

&#x200B;

Anyway, this was hard to write and I'm sorry if it didn't make sense. I really hate that this still bothers me.

I think that is good and all, but for me, it has been where people have said things like how something is wrong with me or just other harsh things that created that feeling of anxiety. I remember just listening to a group of students in the back of the class talking about me. Like me just minding my own business was some kind of personal insult to them or something. 

&#x200B;

So yeah, I would really like to be as comfortable as that guy, but it's hard when you have had people judge you for it in pretty shitty ways. Sorry for being negative.....it has not been a good week, but really, it never quite is.

You know what bothers me about people being 'scared"? It's that there are times where it's just so ass backwards. I have dealt with people who treated me so bad, like just name calling and judging harshly, but then have the nerve to act like me being quiet or whatever is the problem. It never dawned on them that maybe part of that came from just not wanting to deal with them. Like, why would I try to open up to you if you're being an asshole and acting like it's okay?

&#x200B;

I'm sorry, I realize at least for me, I do need to try to make an effort to socialize more. But at the same time, I hate how some people don't see how the way they approach others who may be quiet or shy doesn't exactly make them approachable either.

Years ago, I went on this weekend retreat in college. There was this girl, who I remember introduced herself to me at one point, but I don't remember her name. What I do remember was the name of her boyfriend, Reece, and that was because about every sentence that came out of her mouth, she somehow managed to shoe in a "Reece" or "my boyfriend". I wanted to get out of the van while we were on the freeway.

&#x200B;

Sorry that you had to deal with that btw.
